+## X2 T1 (2026-10-08): exec-equivalent job control
+
+Task X2 T1 (TASK.md S1-S5): under a job-control shell and against third-party stops, `run` must behave like plain exec of the child, on darwin-arm64 and linux-arm64, under bash and zsh. Only `internal/run` changed. `cmd/heimdall-proto` gained tests only.
+
+### What changed and why
+
+| Item | Cause | Change |
+|---|---|---|
+| darwin: a SIGSTOP stop of the child made heimdall-proto exit 255 and leave the child stopped (§9.5; S2, S4, S5) | Go's BSD `syscall.WaitStatus` reports a stop by SIGSTOP as "continued". It compares the stop signal with SIGSTOP, but darwin's WIFCONTINUED marker is 0x13 (SIGCONT). The stop was neither a stop nor an exit, so the loop ended with `ExitStatus()` = 255. | `decodeWait` decodes the raw wait status (same encoding on Linux and darwin). The darwin continued marker and Linux 0xffff are "other", and the loop keeps waiting. |
+| A SIGSTOP stop was reported as SIGTSTP (Linux `$?` 148 instead of 147) | Round 3b mirrored SIGSTOP as SIGTSTP. | The stop is mirrored with the child's own stop signal, so `$?` is 128 + that signal (S2, S4, S5). SIGTTOU still becomes SIGTSTP, because heimdall-proto ignores SIGTTOU. A stop signal heimdall-proto inherited as ignored (`selfIgnores`: `/proc/self/status` SigIgn, darwin `kern.proc.pid` p_sigignore) becomes SIGSTOP. |
+| S1: a non-shell parent, with heimdall-proto able to open a controlling terminal but not in its foreground. A third-party SIGTSTP of the child left heimdall-proto stopped forever, and SIGSTOP exited 255 (darwin). | Every stop was mirrored with `kill(0, sig)`, and nobody continues a stopped heimdall-proto there. | A stop is mirrored only when a job-control shell can see it: our job or the child's group holds the terminal foreground, or a background job's SIGTTIN/SIGTTOU. Otherwise the child is left stopped, as with plain exec, and heimdall-proto keeps waiting. The third party's SIGCONT to the child then lets it finish. |
+| Stale stop reports | A stop already undone by a SIGCONT was still mirrored. | `processStopped` (`/proc/<pid>/stat`, darwin `kern.proc.pid` p_stat) is checked before acting. A stale report is ignored. |
+| Self-stop scope (K6a) | `kill(0, sig)` stops the whole group. | `kill(getpid(), sig)`: only heimdall-proto. |
+| S3: a SIGCONT to heimdall-proto's PID only | (Already worked: the mirror ends on our own SIGCONT and continues the child.) | Kept and tested. The child then re-stops by SIGTTIN on its background read, and heimdall-proto mirrors that as SIGTTIN, as plain exec shows it. |
+| Orphaned group (both terminal modes) | The child's own group is never orphaned, so the kernel does not discard a SIGTSTP/SIGTTIN/SIGTTOU stop of the child, as it does for plain exec in an orphaned group. In modeNoTTY (for example `setsid heimdall-proto run ...`), a third-party SIGTSTP of the child hung the run. | modeNoTTY now also waits with WUNTRACED. In an orphaned group (exact when heimdall-proto is alone in its group, otherwise treated as not orphaned) such a stop is undone with SIGCONT, and a SIGSTOP is left in place. heimdall-proto never mirrors in modeNoTTY. |
+| A SIGCONT received by heimdall-proto outside a mirrored stop was swallowed | Plain exec would have delivered it to the child (for example `kill -CONT %1`). | It is forwarded to the child's group (modeNoTTY and modeExclusive). |
+| A background job whose child a third party stopped, then `fg` | No stop was mirrored (not the foreground job), so the shell believes the job is running. bash's and zsh's `fg` send no SIGCONT to a running job, so the child stayed stopped in the foreground forever. This was a regression risk of the S1 rule. | When the job gets the terminal foreground and a stop of the child was left in place, the handoff poller gives the child the terminal and continues it. |
+| Concurrency | The mutex was held while heimdall-proto was stopped, so nothing else could run. | A `job` struct with a `mirroring` flag guarded by the mutex. The mutex is released while stopped. Our SIGCONT ends the mirror while `mirroring` is set and is forwarded otherwise. The poller leaves the terminal alone during a mirror. |
+
+S2 uses the permitted deviation (b): while heimdall-proto is stopped it cannot see the third party continue the child. The child runs to completion. The shell lists the job stopped until `fg`, which ends it with the child's status.
