+### Known gaps (not in S1-S5)
+
+- A third party stops the child of a background job (a stop no shell sees). The shell lists the job as running, while plain exec shows it stopped. `fg` and `kill -CONT %n` work. `bg` does not continue the child, because the shell sends no SIGCONT to a job it believes is running.
+- A non-shell parent that puts heimdall-proto in the terminal's foreground group (and is not orphaned) would see a third-party stop mirrored, and heimdall-proto would stay stopped. The same applies to a third-party stop of the child while a group the child created (a nested shell's foreground command) holds the terminal: that stop is not mirrored. Both follow from deciding by the terminal foreground alone. Telling a job-control shell from another parent is not possible from inside the job.
+- A SIGCONT that reaches heimdall-proto within microseconds of a child's stop, before heimdall-proto has stopped itself, can end the mirror early. This is unchanged from round 3b.
